WEBVTT

00:00.670 --> 00:01.770
Now ready for part b.

00:01.770 --> 00:04.200
And I've wiped all the
salt off of my fingers,

00:04.200 --> 00:05.450
and I've had a glass of water.

00:05.450 --> 00:07.360
So now I'm ready
for some business.

00:07.360 --> 00:10.640
Find the second derivative
of W with respect

00:10.640 --> 00:15.290
to t in terms of W. Use
this second derivative

00:15.290 --> 00:18.880
to determine whether your answer
in part a is an underestimate

00:18.880 --> 00:21.620
or overestimate for the
amount of solid waste

00:21.620 --> 00:25.490
that the landfill contains
at time t equals 1/4.

00:25.490 --> 00:27.100
So let's do the
first things first.

00:27.100 --> 00:30.440
Let's find the second
derivative in terms of W.

00:30.440 --> 00:32.627
And we already have the
first derivative over here.

00:32.627 --> 00:34.960
And in part a, I rewrote it
just with slightly different

00:34.960 --> 00:35.497
notation.

00:35.497 --> 00:37.830
But I'll just use this just
because I need to write down

00:37.830 --> 00:39.996
here and I can still
refer to this thing.

00:39.996 --> 00:41.370
So let's just take
the derivative

00:41.370 --> 00:44.350
of both sides of this
differential equation

00:44.350 --> 00:45.990
with respect to t.

00:45.990 --> 00:48.160
So you take the derivative
of this left-hand side

00:48.160 --> 00:50.180
with respect to t, you
get the second derivative

00:50.180 --> 00:57.070
of W with respect to t as a
function of t is equal to-- now

00:57.070 --> 00:59.570
you take the derivative
of this with respect to t.

00:59.570 --> 01:04.220
This is the same thing
as 1/25Wt minus 12.

01:04.220 --> 01:05.720
And so when you
take the derivative,

01:05.720 --> 01:07.620
that constant part drops
off, and you're just

01:07.620 --> 01:15.872
left with 1/25 times
the derivative of Wt.

01:15.872 --> 01:16.830
Let me make that clear.

01:16.830 --> 01:26.950
This is the same thing as 1/25W
as a function of t minus 12.

01:26.950 --> 01:29.370
1/25 of 300 is 12.

01:29.370 --> 01:30.910
And you take the
derivative of this,

01:30.910 --> 01:33.810
you get 1/25 times the
first derivative of W

01:33.810 --> 01:36.710
and then the derivative of this
with respect to t is just 0.

01:36.710 --> 01:39.710
A constant, obviously, does
not change with respect to t.

01:39.710 --> 01:41.445
And so we get this
right over here.

01:41.445 --> 01:42.820
Now this is the
second derivative

01:42.820 --> 01:45.072
in terms of the
first derivative.

01:45.072 --> 01:47.280
But the question asks us,
write the second derivative

01:47.280 --> 01:50.260
in terms of W. But
lucky for us, we

01:50.260 --> 01:54.950
know how to express
this as a function of W.

01:54.950 --> 01:57.036
They gave that to
us in the problem.

01:57.036 --> 01:57.660
This was given.

01:57.660 --> 02:00.159
This is just me rewriting
it in a different notation.

02:00.159 --> 02:01.700
So this is going to
be the same thing

02:01.700 --> 02:09.710
as 1/25 times the derivative
of W. And the derivative of W

02:09.710 --> 02:10.462
is this.

02:10.462 --> 02:11.920
The differential
equation literally

02:11.920 --> 02:15.030
tell us the derivative
of W is this over here.

02:15.030 --> 02:22.940
So 1 over 25 times Wt, the
function W as a function of t,

02:22.940 --> 02:26.510
minus 300.

02:26.510 --> 02:29.327
And so we can say the
second derivative--

02:29.327 --> 02:31.660
let me write it a little
smaller so I don't waste space.

02:31.660 --> 02:34.460
The second derivative
of W as a function of t

02:34.460 --> 02:42.840
is equal to 1/625 times
W as a function of t,

02:42.840 --> 02:45.110
which is a function
of t, minus 300.

02:45.110 --> 02:46.650
So we've done the first part.

02:46.650 --> 02:50.710
We've found out the
second derivative of W

02:50.710 --> 02:53.160
in terms of just W.

02:53.160 --> 02:55.980
Now let's try to address the
second part of their question.

02:55.980 --> 02:57.660
So we did the first part.

02:57.660 --> 03:00.220
Now use what we just
figured out to determine

03:00.220 --> 03:02.505
whether your answer in
part a is an underestimate

03:02.505 --> 03:05.490
or an overestimate of
the amount of solid waste

03:05.490 --> 03:08.000
that the landfill
contains at time 1/4.

03:08.000 --> 03:10.090
So in part a, we
found the slope.

03:10.090 --> 03:13.360
We found the slope of the
tangent line at time equals 0,

03:13.360 --> 03:16.010
and we used that slope
to extrapolate out

03:16.010 --> 03:20.070
to 3 months, or time
equals 1/4, 1/4 of a year.

03:20.070 --> 03:23.780
Now if the function
W's slope over that 1/4

03:23.780 --> 03:26.340
was exactly the same as the
slope of the tangent line,

03:26.340 --> 03:28.780
or if that slope did not
change, then our extrapolation

03:28.780 --> 03:30.480
would be exactly right.

03:30.480 --> 03:35.330
If W's slope is
increasing over that time,

03:35.330 --> 03:38.160
then our estimate would
be an underestimate

03:38.160 --> 03:39.470
of the correct thing.

03:39.470 --> 03:41.900
And if the slope is
decreasing over that time,

03:41.900 --> 03:44.990
then our estimate would
be an overestimate

03:44.990 --> 03:47.079
of the actual amount
in the landfill.

03:47.079 --> 03:49.120
And to figure out whether
the slope is increasing

03:49.120 --> 03:50.690
or decreasing, we
just have to look

03:50.690 --> 03:53.070
at the value of the
second derivative.

03:53.070 --> 03:55.300
If the second
derivative is positive,

03:55.300 --> 03:57.264
that means our
slope is increasing,

03:57.264 --> 03:58.930
which means that our
extrapolation would

03:58.930 --> 03:59.997
be an understatement.

03:59.997 --> 04:01.080
Let me just be clear here.

04:01.080 --> 04:02.380
Let me draw this.

04:02.380 --> 04:04.500
Let me make it very clear.

04:04.500 --> 04:07.510
So let me just draw
a random function.

04:07.510 --> 04:13.760
So let's say that's W. So this
is a case where W's slope is

04:13.760 --> 04:16.899
increasing faster,
or W's slope is

04:16.899 --> 04:18.709
increasing from
that starting point.

04:18.709 --> 04:20.990
So our starting point--
this was our slope.

04:20.990 --> 04:24.060
And then W's slope keeps
increasing from there.

04:24.060 --> 04:25.640
And in this case,
our estimate is

04:25.640 --> 04:28.900
going to be underestimate
of where W actually

04:28.900 --> 04:30.840
is after 1/4 of the year.

04:30.840 --> 04:36.230
If W's slope is exactly the same
as our function over the course

04:36.230 --> 04:39.470
of the first three months, it
would look something like that.

04:39.470 --> 04:41.300
Maybe it diverges later on.

04:41.300 --> 04:43.490
And in this case,
our approximation

04:43.490 --> 04:44.850
would be really good.

04:44.850 --> 04:47.380
It would probably
be exact for W.

04:47.380 --> 04:50.010
And if W's slope,
for whatever reason,

04:50.010 --> 04:53.160
goes negative after that point,
and they already tells us

04:53.160 --> 04:57.170
it's an increasing function,
so that is not likely.

04:57.170 --> 04:59.580
Or it could-- it doesn't
have to even go negative.

04:59.580 --> 05:03.139
If W's slope decreases, it
could still stay positive.

05:03.139 --> 05:05.430
And the way I drew it, it
doesn't-- let me draw it like

05:05.430 --> 05:07.690
this, just to make it clear.

05:07.690 --> 05:11.670
So let's say we find out that
the slope looks something

05:11.670 --> 05:13.790
like this.

05:13.790 --> 05:17.380
This is the slope of the
tangent line at time equals 0.

05:17.380 --> 05:20.000
If W's slope increases
from that point,

05:20.000 --> 05:22.440
then w might look
something like that.

05:22.440 --> 05:27.970
And then our answer to part
a would be an understatement,

05:27.970 --> 05:30.500
would be an underestimate
of where W actually

05:30.500 --> 05:31.930
is after a fourth of a year.

05:31.930 --> 05:35.140
Let me draw it and make it
clear to you what I'm doing.

05:35.140 --> 05:39.970
So this is my w-axis.

05:39.970 --> 05:43.320
So this is right at our
initial condition of 1,400.

05:43.320 --> 05:46.350
And then this right over
here is our time axis,

05:46.350 --> 05:48.120
and this is that 1/4.

05:48.120 --> 05:49.960
So in the last
video, we said, hey,

05:49.960 --> 05:53.200
this is sitting right at 1,411.

05:53.200 --> 05:55.660
If W's slope increases
from that point,

05:55.660 --> 05:57.040
then this is an underestimate.

05:57.040 --> 05:59.540
If W's slope stays
the same, then this

05:59.540 --> 06:01.880
is actually a very good
estimate because then we're

06:01.880 --> 06:04.950
going to hit that
point directly with W.

06:04.950 --> 06:08.160
And if W's slope decreases--
so you can imagine maybe

06:08.160 --> 06:11.920
W looks something like this.

06:11.920 --> 06:14.480
It has that slope of the
tangent line when it starts,

06:14.480 --> 06:16.630
but then the slope decreases.

06:16.630 --> 06:18.280
And it's still an
increasing function,

06:18.280 --> 06:19.446
but the slope is decreasing.

06:19.446 --> 06:23.580
And in that case, we would
have an overestimate.

06:23.580 --> 06:27.220
And this is a situation--
so this first situation,

06:27.220 --> 06:28.490
slope is increasing.

06:28.490 --> 06:34.051
That means W prime
prime is positive.

06:34.051 --> 06:35.050
The slope is increasing.

06:35.050 --> 06:38.270
The second derivative
is positive.

06:38.270 --> 06:39.830
This would be a
byproduct of or this

06:39.830 --> 06:43.590
would cause the second
derivative to be negative.

06:43.590 --> 06:45.500
Our slope is decreasing.

06:45.500 --> 06:47.520
And this would be our
second derivative.

06:47.520 --> 06:50.130
Our second derivative is 0.

06:50.130 --> 06:53.089
If your slope isn't changing--
if your slope is constant,

06:53.089 --> 06:54.630
if your first
derivative is constant,

06:54.630 --> 06:57.360
your second derivative
is going to be 0.

06:57.360 --> 06:59.880
So let's just see what
our second derivative is

06:59.880 --> 07:01.600
at our initial
condition, and then we'll

07:01.600 --> 07:03.391
have a pretty good
sense of whether we have

07:03.391 --> 07:06.300
an overestimate or an
underestimate for part a.

07:06.300 --> 07:08.830
So let's just figure out
what our second derivative

07:08.830 --> 07:10.790
at time 0.

07:10.790 --> 07:17.880
It's going to be equal to 1/625
times W of 0, which we know,

07:17.880 --> 07:19.670
minus 300.

07:19.670 --> 07:22.690
Well W of 0, the amount of
waste we have at time 0,

07:22.690 --> 07:26.830
they told us in the
problem, is 1,400 tons.

07:26.830 --> 07:30.940
1,400 minus 1,300 is 1,100.

07:30.940 --> 07:33.970
And then 1,100 minus--
or 1,100 divided by 625

07:33.970 --> 07:35.010
is a small number.

07:35.010 --> 07:35.580
It's 1.

07:35.580 --> 07:36.250
something.

07:36.250 --> 07:37.900
But it is a positive number.

07:37.900 --> 07:39.910
And that's the
important thing here.

07:39.910 --> 07:44.770
So this thing, all of this
business, it is positive.

07:44.770 --> 07:47.130
So the second
derivative is positive,

07:47.130 --> 07:49.040
which means the
slope is increasing.

07:49.040 --> 07:53.220
At least right at our starting
point, our slope is increasing.

07:53.220 --> 07:55.875
Since our slope is increasing
from that point-- and frankly,

07:55.875 --> 07:57.375
the fact that it's
increasing at all

07:57.375 --> 07:59.810
and that we know the W is
an increasing function--

07:59.810 --> 08:04.220
tells us that our estimate in
part a is an underestimate.

08:04.220 --> 08:07.290
So this is the case
that we're dealing with.

08:07.290 --> 08:11.910
So our estimate in part
a is an underestimate

08:11.910 --> 08:15.300
of the actual amount of solid
waste in the landfill at time t

08:15.300 --> 08:17.009
equals 1/4.
